+{
+ "title": "The ONLY 7 Cuts You Need To Tell Any Story (Tim Runia) — transcript de session Warsmith",
+ "source": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J0ydlAqg6_8",
+ "duration_sec": 563,
+ "transcript": [
+  {"timestamp": "0:00", "text": "I used to think that a good edit had a lot of layers, a lot of effects, and even more transitions."},
+  {"timestamp": "0:05", "text": "But after 15 years of being a filmmaker and editing hundreds if not thousands of videos, I realized that great storytelling doesn't come from complicated edits."},
+  {"timestamp": "0:15", "text": "It comes from simple, intentional cuts that actually make the story feel like a story."},
+  {"timestamp": "0:20", "text": "So today I will walk you through the only seven cuts you really need with editing, because these are cuts that I keep coming back to no matter what kind of video I'm making."},
+  {"timestamp": "0:30", "text": "So let's start with one of my favorites. The flow cut."},
+  {"timestamp": "0:33", "text": "This is when your shots feel like they're dancing together. It's smooth and invisible."},
+  {"timestamp": "0:39", "text": "When something moves in your frame, like a person or an object, or it could even be the camera itself, your viewer's eyes start to follow that motion."},
+  {"timestamp": "0:48", "text": "And if your next shot picks up that movement in the same direction, their eyes just keep going effortlessly."},
+  {"timestamp": "1:08", "text": "If instead the first motion goes up and the next shot starts going to the opposite corner or going sideways, that's friction and the viewer will feel the cut."},
+  {"timestamp": "1:20", "text": "Start thinking of editing a bit more like choreography. If you guide the eyes naturally, the viewer won't notice the cuts and they will just stay inside of the story."},
+  {"timestamp": "1:42", "text": "Where the flow cut is really about smooth transitions, this one is about disruption actually."},
+  {"timestamp": "1:48", "text": "A smash cut is when you intentionally break the rhythm. It's a sudden cut from one scene to a completely different one."},
+  {"timestamp": "1:57", "text": "And it often works best when music or the sound cuts out too, because that really grabs attention."},
+  {"timestamp": "2:03", "text": "And it creates contrast and it tells the viewer, hey, pay attention, because something just changed."},
+  {"timestamp": "2:27", "text": "What makes this cut really powerful is the emotional reset it adds. And this can add humor or tension or simply just keep the viewer from falling into a predictable rhythm."},
+  {"timestamp": "2:45", "text": "Just don't overdo it because, you know, surprise only works if it's, well, surprising."},
+  {"timestamp": "3:07", "text": "But now let's talk about the jump cut."},
+  {"timestamp": "3:11", "text": "The jump cut gets a bad reputation because it's used like this in talking head videos. But it's actually one of the most practical cuts there is because you're staying in the same shots, but you skip forward in time."},
+  {"timestamp": "3:30", "text": "This is also one of those cuts that I really like to land on exactly the beat. Because that just feels good sometimes."},
+  {"timestamp": "3:39", "text": "So it's a great way to show progress repetition or speed something up."},
+  {"timestamp": "3:54", "text": "So the action cut is all about editing in the middle of movement."},
+  {"timestamp": "3:58", "text": "So when you cut during an action, like sitting down for example, the motion continues across the cut. It feels smooth and it feels natural because your viewer's brain is already following the movement."},
+  {"timestamp": "4:24", "text": "The key here is again to guide the viewer's eyes. If their attention stays on the same spot in the frame, the cut becomes almost invisible."},
+  {"timestamp": "4:43", "text": "And sometimes, you know, just a few frames earlier or later can really make the difference. So really pay attention to that."},
+  {"timestamp": "5:01", "text": "So yeah, this is actually not a single cut. It's more like a sequence of cuts that work together so you can guide the viewer through a moment."},
+  {"timestamp": "5:13", "text": "I'm talking about the wide medium close pattern."},
+  {"timestamp": "5:17", "text": "So you start with a wide shot to show us where we are, then a medium to show what's happening, and then a closeup to show details or show emotion."},
+  {"timestamp": "5:30", "text": "And this gives your story structure and it gives your audience clarity."},
+  {"timestamp": "5:44", "text": "Maybe you will skip the medium entirely for a more of emotional kind of hit going from wide to close immediately."},
+  {"timestamp": "5:51", "text": "Or you flip it around and you start close to only reveal the full picture in the end to surprise the viewer."},
+  {"timestamp": "6:16", "text": "So this is one of the most satisfying cuts when you pull it off right. It's called a match cut."},
+  {"timestamp": "6:21", "text": "And it connects two shots using something visually similar. So that could be a shape or subject in the same position within the frame."},
+  {"timestamp": "6:35", "text": "So it can link moments in time or places, or you can use it to create metaphors."},
+  {"timestamp": "6:55", "text": "And if you're matching people, there's one trick that's really important to remember, and it's that you try to match the eye position across the cut."},
+  {"timestamp": "7:02", "text": "Because when the eyes stay in the same place on the screen, the transition will feel way more natural."},
+  {"timestamp": "7:15", "text": "You know, sometimes the most important part of a cut is when it actually happens."},
+  {"timestamp": "7:20", "text": "Editing is rhythm. And if your video moves at the same pace the whole time, your audience will sure start to tune out."},
+  {"timestamp": "7:28", "text": "So paying attention to the speed of your cuts is how you keep them engaged by shifting the tempo."},
+  {"timestamp": "7:33", "text": "The faster cuts here are building tension, and the slower cuts are there to let something land emotionally."},
+  {"timestamp": "7:41", "text": "It's all about contrast. Just like music, it needs climax, and it needs a release."},
+  {"timestamp": "7:46", "text": "And I use this when I want to change the energy of a scene or guide the viewer into how I want them to feel: exciting or calm or emotional or chaotic."},
+  {"timestamp": "8:57", "text": "You don't need to cram all these seven cuts into one video. Just ask yourself, what does this moment need? Does it need a smooth flow, a surprise, or a shift in rhythm maybe?"},
+  {"timestamp": "9:12", "text": "From there, you can always build further."}
+ ],
+ "key_techniques": [
+  "flow_cut",
+  "smash_cut",
+  "jump_cut",
+  "action_cut",
+  "wide_medium_close",
+  "match_cut",
+  "pacing_cut",
+  "eye_position_match",
+  "sound_cut_with_smash",
+  "cut_on_beat"
+ ]
+}
